Sam Potter, April 2024, former Courant Instructor of Greengard
%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
	A problem with an EGC solution?
	Sam Potter <sam@coreform.com>
	Apr 18, 2024, 4:24 PM (7 days ago)
	Dear Prof. Yap,
	
	(Sorry about the long email---no rush in responding to this...)
	
	I work as a research scientist at Coreform, LLC, a company that
	specializes in finite elements and isogeometric analysis, but in reality
	does quite a lot of work in meshing and geometry. Actually, I was at
	CIMS the past few years working with Leslie Greengard as a CI, but have
	since left academia for industry...
	
	I was somewhat aware of your group's work while I was at NYU, and since
	starting at Coreform I've come across many, many problems that seem like
	a good fit for your EGC paradigm. Right now we do our best with epsilon
	hacking and twiddling, but there is only so far you can go with this.
	
	I'm interested in assessing whether EGC is in a state where it can be
	deployed by practicioners on an ad hoc/daily basis (i.e., is it worth
	our time to develop some expertise in it, or is it too abtruse and not
	ready for "prime time"). Rather than read many papers that are outside
	my field, I thought I would go to the source...
	
	To that end, I have a self-contained problem I thought I would float by
	you. If you don't have time for this, please just let me know.
	
	Here it is: say you're interested in contouring a smooth level set
	phi(x, y) = 0. We lay down a regular, square background mesh on a region
	fully containing the level set. To start the contouring process, we pick
	a vertex from this background mesh, and ask whether the vertex lies on
	the level set. Later, we wll consider each edge incident on that vertex,
	approximate phi's restriction to that edge with a Chebyshev series, and
	solve an eigenvalue problem to find all real roots of that Chebyshev
	series to find where it cuts each edge.
	
	If the vertex is cut by the level set, then the four incident edges are
	likely all cut in approximately the same place. The vagaries of floating
	point arithmetic seem to indicate that we could find between 0 and 5
	cuts when the level set "truly" cuts the vertex.
		Is approaching this tangle more robustly doable with EGA?
		Thanks very much for your time. Any insight into this problem will be
	greatly appreciated.
		Regards, Sam Potter
