	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
	First Email in May 2017:
	%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%%
	Dr. Yap,
	
	(my apologies if this email is a duplicate - I initially attempted to
	send it to the exact@cs.nyu.edu email address but got a delivery
	failed message when it expanded to pion@cs.nyu.edu...)
	
	Hi!  My name is Cliff Yapp, and I work on an open source CAD system
	called BRL-CAD.
	
	I've recently been digging into modern research concerning robust
	geometric calculations, and the EGC work came up in that search.
	Although the EGC Introduction website indicates post 2009 research is
	looking at moving beyond EGC due to performance concerns, I'm
	wondering if EGC might serve as a useful "slow-but-working" fallback
	for a number of features in BRL-CAD which currently suffer from
	robustness problems.
	
	Looking at the CORE historical distributions, it looks as if the
	choice of the QPL license was made circa 2004.  Although I've not
	found anything indicating the specific reason for that choice, I'm
	wondering if it was driven by a desire to match the CGAL licensing
	scheme of that time?  If so, is there any possibility that at least
	the "core" bits of CORE could be relicensed under a more liberal
	license such as Apache or MIT (or, if those are too liberal, perhaps
	the MPL2 ?)
	
	I hasten to state that if the choice of the QPL is retained
	deliberately (either for philosophical reasons or to preserve the
	possibility of commercial licensing) I fully respect that decision.
	However, before I start diving into trying to create my own
	implementation of the necessary robust primitives, I figured it was
	worth inquiring as to whether or not the selection of QPL was
	deliberate or simply a matter of convenience/default when working with
	CGAL.  I've encountered both situations before, so it's usually worth
	checking.
	
	Thanks for your time,
	CY
	
